A||||1||GT Bastard||Drove an Isuzu 5-sp behind a V6 yesterday...||||14||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||06-22-2000||03:46 AM||||A friend of mine just completed an Isuzu 5-sp swap into his '86 SE V6.  Damn, it's like driving a Honda.  It shifts smooth as butter, but it doesn't quite seem to have the raw acceleration that it once did.  At any rate, the car felt really smooth.  If you want economy and smooth shifting, I think it's great, but personally I'd stick with a 4-sp.  Just my 2 cents...||||reg||14
Z||000001||HYPTNOTiSE||06-22-2000||12:34 PM||||Hey GT bastard, i've got the best of both worlds about to be installed in my car.  I took an isuzu 5 speed, and dumped the 3.35 gears for 3.83 gears.  The numbers on top designate the gear ratio for each gear, the higher #'s on bottom are a porduct of the specific gear multiplied by the final drive ratio. Higher number means better acceleration.<P>Fiero isuzu 5speed- 3.35 final drive<BR>1        2       3        4       5<BR>3.73	2.04	1.45	1.03	0.74<BR>12.50	6.83	4.86	3.45	2.48<P>Fiero isuzu 5speed- 3.83 final drive<BR>1        2       3       4       5<BR>3.73	2.04	1.45	1.03	0.74<BR>14.29	7.81	5.55	3.94	2.83	<P>Fiero 4 speed- 3.65 final drive<BR>1        2       3       4<BR>3.31	1.95	1.24	0.81<BR>12.08	7.12	4.53	2.96<P>Fiero 4 speed- 4.10 final drive<BR>1       2         3      4<BR>3.53	1.95	1.24	0.81<BR>14.47	8.00	5.08	3.32<P>So now i have a super smooth shifting tranny, with 1st and 2nd gears almost as low as the famed 4.10, 3rd and 4th are lower and 5th is better for cruisin than the 3.65 4 speed was.  <BR>It's not in the car yet, but i'll let everyone know what the car runs.<BR>	||||reg||1
